{
  "regeln": [
    "Visual Evidence: Show genuine source-supported visuals for every tool action; replace generic desk/office scenes with specific UI, terminal outputs, or code showing the task (e.g., text extraction).",
    "Technical Proof: Provide visible evidence for all technical claims; if 'No GPU' or 'No PyTorch' is mentioned, show the installation command, dependencies, or terminal logs as proof.",
    "Output Clarity: Display authentic tool results such as structured JSON, Markdown, or text boxes instead of pixelated logos, generic warnings, or abstract graphics.",
    "Narration Parity: Maintain strict parity between narration and imagery; every technical step, UI element, or data point mentioned must be visibly represented at that exact moment.",
    "Realistic Workflows: Prioritize real software screen recordings and active workflows over text-only graphics, titles, or abstract charts.",
    "Distinct Motifs: Ensure every visual change introduces a materially different subject or action; zooms, crops, label changes, or backgrounds do not count as new motifs.",
    "Functional Hook: Open scripts with a clear, user-focused hook that states the practical task the viewer will accomplish rather than generic trending-tools questions.",
    "Practical Explanation: Explain technical trade-offs and limits in plain language; do not use architecture names or benchmarks as a substitute for explaining the human task.",
    "Format Integrity: Preserve the channel's painted-world style for fictional scenes and maintain the recurring presenter's identity (black hair, leather jacket, cyan glasses).",
    "Factual Accuracy: Never override source-supported facts or approved voice-overs; show UI buttons and data pathways exactly as they appear in the source.",
    "Mandatory CTA: Include the spoken CTA (like, share, save) at the format-specific position; do not substitute alternative calls-to-action.",
    "Data-Driven Fixes: Treat review feedback as provisional data; apply concrete, observable fixes to specific segments without overgeneralizing a single defect into a ban on a style."
  ],
